26: \begin{abstract}
27: \noindent
28: Drag coefficients are reported for three smooth circular cylinders with nominal diameters of 25.4, 38.1 and 50.8~mm towed through a 0.90~m wide, 0.75~m deep laboratory tank. Each cylinder was tested at ten speeds between 0.2 and 1.1~m/s, giving Reynolds numbers from $4.7\times10^{3}$ to $5.0\times10^{4}$ based on the measured water temperature. Drag force was measured with a calibrated load cell and averaged over a 5~s steady window. After rejecting one run affected by load-cell zero drift, the mean drag coefficient is $C_D = 1.150 \pm 0.030$ (mean $\pm$ standard deviation, $n=29$), with cylinder means of 1.143, 1.152 and 1.157 for the 25.4, 38.1 and 50.8~mm cylinders, respectively. The three cylinder means agree within 1.2\%; the weak Reynolds-number trends in the two smaller cylinders are comparable to the condition-to-condition scatter and are not interpreted as a physical dependence. The streamwise force is dominated by a tone at twice the vortex-shedding frequency, corresponding to a Strouhal number of $0.200 \pm 0.003$, which supports the quality of the steady window. The results are consistent with the classical subcritical drag coefficient of about 1.2. Correcting for water temperature changes the Reynolds number by up to 17\% but the drag coefficient by less than 0.2\%. Blockage ratios of 2.3--4.5\% imply corrections of a few percent, largest for the 2~in cylinder. The dataset and processing scripts are provided to support reproducibility and future comparisons.
