127: \begin{figure}[t]
128: \centering
129: \includegraphics[width=0.78\linewidth]{figures/fig2_cd_vs_re.pdf}
130: \caption{Drag coefficient against Reynolds number for the three cylinders. Error bars are the propagated per-run uncertainty from Eq.~\eqref{eq:unc}. The open cross marks run~17, which was excluded because of load-cell zero drift. The dashed line is the commonly quoted subcritical value $C_D \approx 1.2$.}
131: \label{fig:cd}
132: \end{figure}
133: 
134: Figure~\ref{fig:cd} shows $C_D$ against $Re$. A least-squares fit of $C_D$ to $\log_{10}Re$ gives slopes of $+0.11 \pm 0.07$, $+0.07 \pm 0.06$ and $-0.01 \pm 0.12$ per decade for the 1, 1.5 and 2~in cylinders, respectively (95\% confidence intervals). The two smaller cylinders therefore show a weak positive trend of about 5--8\% across the tested range, while the 2~in data show no resolved trend. Because these trends are comparable to the scatter and are opposite in sign to the slow decrease usually reported in the subcritical regime, we do not interpret them as a physical Reynolds-number dependence; they are more likely to reflect facility effects that grow with speed (see \S\ref{sec:scatter}). Over the present range, the data are consistent with a constant $C_D \approx 1.15$.
135: 
136: \subsection{Temperature and blockage sensitivity}
137: \label{sec:blockage}
138: 
139: Using the measured water temperature instead of a fixed 20~\degreeC\ value changes $Re$ by $+5.7\%$ to $+17.2\%$, with the largest change for the coldest (March) runs. The effect on $C_D$ is negligible ($<0.2\%$), because $\rho$ varies by less than 1\% over the temperature range and the force measurement is unaffected. Temperature correction is therefore important for placing the data on the correct $Re$ axis but not for the drag coefficient itself.
140: 
141: A Maskell-type blockage correction \cite{barlow1999,maskell1963} of the form $q_c/q = 1 + \theta C_D B$, with $C_{D,c} = C_D/(q_c/q)$, was applied as a sensitivity test. For $\theta = 1$, appropriate to a two-dimensional bluff body, the corrected cylinder means are 1.114, 1.108 and 1.099 for the 1, 1.5 and 2~in cylinders; for $\theta = 2.5$, appropriate to a three-dimensional bluff body, they are 1.074, 1.049 and 1.023. The correction is 2.5--11\%, largest for the 2~in cylinder. Because the appropriate $\theta$ for a finite-length cylinder piercing a free surface in a tow tank is not established, we report uncorrected values as the primary result and treat the corrected values as a systematic uncertainty. The correction is large enough that it could account for part or all of the apparent increase of $C_D$ with diameter.
142: 
143: \section{Discussion}
144: 
145: \subsection{Comparison with published data}
146: 
147: The present mean, $C_D = 1.150$, is about 4\% below the commonly quoted subcritical value of 1.2 \cite{schlichting2017,zdravkovich1997}. Given the $\pm10\%$ spread among published measurements and the few-percent blockage correction, the agreement is reasonable. The data do not reach the drag crisis: the maximum Reynolds number is $5.0\times10^{4}$, roughly a factor of four below the critical Reynolds number of about $2\times10^{5}$ at which $C_D$ begins to fall. The observed Strouhal number of 0.200 is also the classical subcritical value \cite{norberg2003,roshko1961}.
148: 
149: \subsection{Sources of scatter and limitations}
150: \label{sec:scatter}
151: 
152: The standard deviation across the ten conditions within each cylinder (2.3--2.9\%) is larger than the propagated uncertainty (1.3--1.4\%). Several facility effects can contribute: (i) tow-speed unsteadiness and carriage vibration, which enter $C_D$ quadratically; (ii) free-surface waves and air entrainment, especially for cylinders that pierce the surface; (iii) finite-length and end effects, with $L/D = 23.6$, 15.7 and 11.8 for the three cylinders; (iv) blockage, which is largest for the 2~in cylinder; (v) slow zero drift of the load cell, which affected run~17; and (vi) temperature stratification and drift between runs. The single run per condition means that these effects cannot be separated from true condition-to-condition variability.
153: 
154: Run~17 (2~in cylinder, $U = 0.8$~m/s) was excluded. Its zero measured at the end of the 12~s record was 84~mV (4.2~N equivalent) above the pre-run tare, and its steady-window force drifted from 12.6~N (3--5~s) to 14.0~N (7--8~s). The resulting $C_D = 1.355$ is 17\% above the cylinder mean. Including it would raise the 2~in mean from 1.157 to 1.176 and increase its standard deviation from 0.034 to 0.070. Run~4 (1~in cylinder, $U = 0.5$~m/s) was recorded immediately after a run that produced a small free-surface wave; its $C_D = 1.119$ is within the scatter of the other 1~in runs, so it was retained.
155: 
156: The main limitations of the dataset are: three diameters at a single immersion; one run per condition; no independent measurement of carriage speed or vibration; no characterization of surface roughness or cylinder roundness; no separate measurement of the drag of the submerged support, which may contribute to the measured force; no lift or wake measurements; and no experimentally validated blockage correction for this tank. The absolute uncertainty of the reported $C_D$ is therefore a few percent, even though the relative precision within a campaign is better.
157: 
158: \subsection{Recommendations for future work}
159: 
160: Repeat runs at each condition would separate facility variability from the mean trend. An independent measurement of carriage speed (for example, from an encoder or timing gates) would reduce the dominant uncertainty in $C_D$. Surface roughness and diameter should be measured for each cylinder. Varying the immersion depth would quantify free-surface and end effects, and a blockage correction should be validated for this tank geometry, either with a standard method or with a companion CFD study. Extending the Reynolds-number range towards the drag crisis, and adding wake surveys or particle image velocimetry, would connect the present mean-force data to the underlying flow structure.
161: 
162: \section{Conclusions}
163: 
164: Drag coefficients have been measured for three smooth circular cylinders in a tow tank over $Re = 4.7\times10^{3}$--$5.0\times10^{4}$. After rejecting one run affected by load-cell zero drift, the mean is $C_D = 1.150 \pm 0.030$ ($n=29$). The cylinder means agree within 1.2\%, and the weak Reynolds-number trends in the two smaller cylinders are comparable to the condition-to-condition scatter rather than a clear physical dependence. The streamwise force is dominated by a tone at twice the vortex-shedding frequency, with an implied Strouhal number of $0.200 \pm 0.003$, consistent with the classical subcritical value. Temperature correction is important for the Reynolds number (up to 17\%) but not for the drag coefficient ($<0.2\%$). Blockage ratios of 2.3--4.5\% imply corrections of a few percent, largest for the 2~in cylinder; these should be validated before the absolute values are used for comparison with other facilities.
